From 13a4e50d3599173b01d4b765ac6c57b007ad132b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Z User <z@container>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 20:09:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] asmdiff/encdiff: stateful disassembly grammar, one-pin
 rows, digest casing

Re-base of the unpushed S15-a half (PR #736 audit findings 2, 3, 6) onto
current main: the tool blobs were identical to the patch's pre-images, so
asmdiff.py/encdiff.py carry the content verbatim; the test files merge the
stateful-grammar/one-pin/digest-casing cases into the W6-pinned versions
(the pinned-tool channels and require-mode from PR #738/#743 kept).

- _parse_disasm (asmdiff) and dis() (encdiff.decodes_same) now parse the
  REAL pinned-2.47 listing grammar with full state: anchor byte columns
  are a fixed width (21 on x86, inferred per listing, consistency
  enforced), continuation lines are admitted only when the previous line
  FILLED the column (objdump wraps only on overflow), their address is
  the RUNNING address, and the accumulated instruction stays within the
  15-byte architectural maximum -- the byte-accounting invariant, which
  also holds between consecutive anchors and is verified per symbol
  block. Orphan byte fragments (the audit repro: two listings differing
  only in an orphan ff/ee both parsed to ['nop']) now refuse the whole
  stream; encdiff's parser additionally stops silently SKIPPING unknown
  line shapes (two streams differing only in skipped lines could compare
  equal). A wrapped instruction's TRUE length (anchor + continuations,
  from the verified chain) now feeds _branch_marker, so a wrapped branch
  is target-compared with its real end address, not the column width.

- split_byte_pin (encdiff) enforces EXACTLY ONE pin-shaped tag per row:
  the whole comment is scanned for tag occurrences (any case, any
  near-miss separator) before grammar is applied. '# byte-exact 90
  # byte-exact 91' used to pin 91 with the conflicting 90 silently
  ignored (a $-anchored search finds the last valid tag); repeated
  identical tags, malformed+valid pairs and near-miss spellings are all
  hard errors now.

- The rows-sha256 baseline digest is normalised to lowercase at the
  parse boundary: the grammar was case-insensitive but the comparison
  was verbatim against hashlib's lowercase hexdigest, so an uppercase
  spelling of the CORRECT digest parsed fine and reported drift forever.

Validated on this box: test_asmdiff 23/23 and test_encdiff 69/69 each
bare AND pinned+require (real-toolchain legs verified to run, incl. the
wrapped-instruction leg); require-mode proven to error on a bad pin.
Corpus gates re-run after the compiler build (see the series battery).
